# Scan-set contract for the cross-reference gate.
#
# READ BY `cargo xtask lint-references` (xtask/src/lint/contract.rs). It is the one
# enumeration definition the gate consumes, and it exists because there was none: the two
# shell ports this gate replaced each swept their own list — one `find src tests sdd
# scripts packaging gtktest data . -maxdepth 4`, whose bare `.` made check 6b repo-wide,
# the other seven named directories plus four named files. `.agents/`, `docs/` and
# `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md` were therefore linted on Linux and invisible on Windows, so a
# dangling link in any of them failed one gate and passed the other.
#
# IT STAYS A DATA FILE NOW THAT THERE IS ONE IMPLEMENTATION, and the reason is not
# inertia. The set is a policy statement about what this repository is responsible for —
# which trees, which loose files, which prefixes are generated rather than authored, which
# documents a developer obeys. Moving it into Rust would make every such decision a code
# change reviewed as code, and would put the enumeration back inside the thing it is meant
# to constrain.
#
# ONE SET, CONSUMED BY EVERY CHECK. `root` and `file` together define a single
# enumeration, produced exactly once; every check narrows it rather than walking the tree
# again. A check that walks for itself is a second enumeration, and there is no such thing
# as a harmless one — three coexisted here and disagreed with each other (ScrAP-207). What
# each check narrows to is its own business and is documented at the check: 6a scans the
# whole set, 6b its `.md` members, check 1 everything but the register, checks 2 and 5 the
# `src/` members.
#
# THREE CLASSES OF ENTRY, and the differences matter:
#
#   root / file — content this repository is responsible for.
#
#   exclude     — a repo-relative path PREFIX removed from the set. These are GENERATED or
#                 VENDORED trees, and the reason to exclude them is not tidiness: every one
#                 is gitignored, so whether it is present depends on what the developer
#                 happened to run locally. Sweeping them makes the gate's verdict depend on
#                 the machine rather than on the commit — `docs/` holds QA review
#                 artifacts, `.agents/skills` holds installed third-party skill documents
#                 whose links this project does not own, and `tests/reports/` holds
#                 manual-test output absent on a fresh clone.
#
#   prescriptive — a LABEL ON A SET MEMBER: a document a reader could ACT on, scanned by
#                 check 5b for prose that instructs the very attribute check 5 rejects.
#                 It is a label rather than a reuse of `file` because the two answer
#                 different questions: `file` means "this repository is responsible for the
#                 links in it", `prescriptive` means "a developer obeys what it says". The
#                 gate REFUSES TO RUN if a path marked here is not in the scan set. That
#                 check is the difference between a label and a fourth enumeration: without
#                 it, 5b read a list that was neither the bounded nor the unbounded set.
#                 `sdd/ANTI-PATTERNS.md` is emphatically in the `file` set and not this one
#                 — the register DESCRIBES the banned attribute (it is an entry title) and
#                 scanning it produced thirteen failures on correct text. A gate that cries
#                 wolf on correct text is a gate someone deletes.
#
# Deliberately NOT a `git ls-files` enumeration: a tracked-only list silently skips a
# document that has been written but not yet added, which is exactly when its links are
# least likely to have been checked. (Check 12 is the deliberate exception and takes
# `git ls-files` precisely because its hazard is wider than this curated set.)
#
# SYMLINKS ARE EXCLUDED — and the mechanism is not the obvious one.
#
# A symlinked path must never enter the scan set, because the two platforms do not read the
# same bytes through one. Measured, not assumed, on tests/fixtures/escapes-via-symlink.md
# (git mode 120000 -> ../other-fixtures/outside-doc.md): on Linux a symlink handed to a
# reader AS AN EXPLICIT PATH is FOLLOWED, so the target is scanned twice under two names
# and any dangler inside it is reported twice. On a Windows checkout with
# core.symlinks=false git materialises the link as an ordinary 32-byte text file whose
# entire content is the target PATH, so the gate lints a string nobody authored — no
# symlink semantics are involved at all, which is why no filesystem test can catch it
# there. Same commit, same path, different content. Excluding the link costs no coverage —
# the target is independently in the set via its own root
# (tests/other-fixtures/outside-doc.md) — and that, not tidiness, is why this is safe.
#
# The mechanism must be the git INDEX MODE (120000) as well as a filesystem test, and this
# is the trap: each platform's filesystem test is blind in a different way. A `-type l`
# test sees the link on Linux and nothing on a core.symlinks=false checkout, where the
# attributes are those of a plain file — so porting it to its natural Windows equivalent
# (LinkType / ReparsePoint / ResolveLinkTarget) excludes NOTHING while reading as parity.
# The index mode is the only property both platforms agree on, because it is git's record
# rather than the OS's. Test BOTH (filesystem OR index): each covers the other's blind
# spot, and the union degrades to "include" only when both are blind, which is the safe
# direction — it can never silently skip an authored document.
#
# Consequence to accept knowingly: the symlink CLASSIFICATION consults git, though the
# ENUMERATION still does not. Outside a git checkout (an exported tarball) the index half
# is unavailable and only the filesystem half applies; that degrades to today's Linux
# behaviour rather than to a wrong answer. We do not run the gate that way.
#
# There is deliberately no `symlink` FIELD below: the rule takes no value, so a field would
# be a constant the validator must learn for no gain. It is pinned by the corpus in
# `xtask/src/lint/corpus.rs`, which asserts that every mode-120000 path is absent from the
# scan set AND that at least one such path exists — the second half matters, because if the
# fixture is ever deleted the first half starts passing vacuously, which is the exact
# defect this project already paid for once.
#
# KNOW WHAT THAT ASSERTION DOES NOT PROVE ON LINUX. It tests the OUTCOME (no symlink in the
# set), not the MECHANISM, and on Linux a `-type f` walk already produces that outcome by
# itself. Verified by mutation: neutering the exclusion and re-running the corpus still
# PASSES; it only fails if the enumeration is ALSO broadened to include link entries. So on
# this platform the exclusion is unfalsifiable dead code and could be deleted with every
# gate staying green. That is not a defect in the assertion — asserting the outcome is
# right, because Windows reaches the same outcome ONLY via the index half, which is where
# it is load-bearing and where the mutation does fail. It is a warning: do not read a green
# Linux run as evidence that this code works, and do not "simplify it away" on the strength
# of one.
#
# maxdepth IS A TRIPWIRE, NOT A FILTER. Exceeding it is a HARD ERROR: the gate refuses to
# run and names every offending path. Raise the budget here, or move the files.
#
# As a filter it was a silent coverage cliff, and the cliff is what made one shared set
# unsafe. A file past the budget was simply absent, and "absent because the budget dropped
# it" is indistinguishable from "absent because the tree does not contain it"; binding
# every check to one enumeration would therefore have REDUCED what check 1 caught,
# silently, which is the exact shape of the defects this file exists to close. Failing
# loudly inverts that: the budget stops being a cliff and becomes a statement that the tree
# has outgrown the contract.
#
# It is expressed in `find` terms, where `find <root> -maxdepth 1` means the root's
# immediate children — depth is counted from the repo-relative PATH, as segments below the
# root, rather than from the walker. `file` entries are named individually rather than
# reached by a walk, so they match no root prefix and are never depth-counted.
#
# WHAT THE CORPUS ASSERTS about this file, each assertion able to fail on its own and each
# mutation-tested against the defect it exists to catch:
#   - a file at exactly `maxdepth` is present in the scan set;
#   - a file at `maxdepth`+1 makes the gate FAIL and names the path — not merely "is absent
#     from the set", because absence is also what a broken enumerator produces;
#   - a garbled field name is a refusal to run rather than an absent value, since "absent"
#     and "legitimately empty" are indistinguishable and the gate would run over nothing.

root src
root tests
root sdd
root scripts
root packaging
root gtktest
root data
# The hand-authored sections of THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md. They are INPUTS to
# `src/notices.rs`'s generator, so the generated output at the repo root is not itself
# authored — but these are, they carry links and citations like any other prose we own,
# and content the tree is responsible for that no gate can see is the exact gap this
# contract exists to close. Named as a root rather than a file so a new section is
# covered the moment it is added, which is the whole point of the sections being files.
root notices
# The gate's own crate. It is a root for the reason every other one is — a gate is its
# pattern AND its file set, and the implementation of the gate is exactly the place a
# citation or a plan pointer gets written and then rots. One file inside it,
# `xtask/src/lint/corpus.rs`, is excluded by checks 1, 6a and 8 in code, because it quotes
# the citation forms they hunt for; that is a linter's own fixtures, and it is ONE path
# rather than the pair the shell ports had to keep in step by hand.
root xtask
# `probes/` holds measurement rigs and their README — C sources, a nested Rust workspace,
# and prose that cites the registers. Depth 2 (the nested crate's src/ costs a segment),
# well inside the maxdepth tripwire.
root probes

file AGENTS.md
file README.md
file Cargo.toml
file build.rs
# Both carry ANTI-PATTERNS citations in comments and neither was in the set, so the
# citation-form gate (check 8) could not see them — `.cargo/config.toml` is the
# process-wide `[env]` choke point and `clippy.toml` the method-ban list, which is
# exactly where a lesson gets cited. Named as `file` entries rather than adding a
# `root .cargo` so no dot-directory is walked. Found by grepping the WHOLE tracked
# tree for the banned form and comparing against what the gate reported: a gate is
# its pattern AND its set, and the two disagreed here in the set's direction.
file .cargo/config.toml
file clippy.toml
# The pinned toolchain, and it is here for the reason its two neighbours are: its header
# cites this project's register while explaining WHY the version is frozen, and a citation
# the gate cannot see is one no sweep will ever correct. It also states the cost of the
# current pin, which is exactly the kind of claim that rots the moment the value moves.
file rust-toolchain.toml
# The CI workflow, for the same reason as the two above: it cites this project's register
# in comments, and a citation the gate cannot see is one no sweep will ever correct. Named
# as a `file` entry rather than adding a `root .github` so no dot-directory is walked —
# the same treatment `.cargo/config.toml` gets, and for the same reason.
file .github/workflows/pipeline.yml

prescriptive sdd/POLICY.md
prescriptive sdd/TDD.md
prescriptive AGENTS.md
prescriptive README.md

exclude target/
exclude build/
exclude docs/
exclude .git/
exclude .agents/
exclude .claude/
exclude tests/reports/
# GENERATED by build.rs and gitignored, so whether it is present depends on whether the
# developer has built -- exactly the machine-dependence this class exists to remove. Its
# authored INPUTS are swept instead, via `root notices` above.
exclude THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md
# Check 14's fixtures are DELIBERATELY malformed -- one is a BOM-less .ps1 carrying an
# em dash, which is the exact condition check 14 refuses. They are excluded from the
# scan set so they do not fail the gate they exist to prove, and the corpus test reaches
# them by PATH, through check 14's own predicate, rather than through this set.
exclude tests/fixtures/encoding/
# A nested crate's build tree, and the reason it needs its own line rather than being
# covered by `exclude target/` above: the exclusions are repo-relative path PREFIXES, and
# `probes/binding-shape-rs/target/` does not begin with `target/`. Adding `probes` as a
# root therefore brought that crate's build output into the set for anyone who has ever
# built the rig, and cargo's own artefacts go deeper than the maxdepth budget, so the gate
# refuses to run and names a `.o` file. Measured by the macOS seat on a real
# `cargo build --manifest-path probes/binding-shape-rs/Cargo.toml`, and reproduced here by
# hand. It is the same gitignored-tree reasoning as every exclusion above — the verdict
# must depend on the commit, not on what the developer happened to build — and it changes
# no scan set on a clean checkout, where the directory does not exist.
exclude probes/binding-shape-rs/target/
# The gate's own build tree, for the same reason: `xtask/target/` does not begin with
# `target/` either, and `cargo` puts a workspace member's artefacts in the shared root
# `target/` only while it IS a member — a stray build with `--manifest-path` does not.
exclude xtask/target/

maxdepth 6
